Note, if Pt0p0q  fpt0q reduces to a constant, the Holder exponent characterises the power law behaviour of the
increments at t0
|fpt0   θq  fpt0q| ¤ Cpt0q|θ|hf pt0q
where Cpt0q is called the prefactor at time t0. Put another way, the exponent hfpt0q quantiﬁes the scaling properties
of the process at time t0. The Holder exponent generalises the heuristic deﬁnition of singularity, so that it is also
called the singularity exponent. That is, if f has Holder exponent hpt0q  hfpt0q   1 at t0, then f has at t0 either a
cusp-type singularity
|fpt0   θq  fpt0q|  C|θ|hpt0q
(11.1.15)
or an oscillating singularity
|fpt0   θq  fpt0q|  C|θ|hpt0q sin p 1
|θ|β q
(11.1.16)
with oscillation exponent β ¡ 0. Conversely, if f has either a cusp or an oscillating singularity at t0 and 1 ¡ hpt0q,
then hpt0q  hfpt0q is the Holder exponent of f at t0.
Multifractal situations happen when the Holder exponent is no-longer unique, but vary from point to point. More
precisely, when the regularity hptq is itself a highly irregular function of t, possibly even a random process rather
than a constant or a ﬁxed deterministic function, the process X is said to be multifractal. The aim of multifractal
analysis is to provide a description of the collection of Holder exponents h of the function f. Since the Holder
exponent may jump from one point to another, it was understood that describing them for each time step was not of
much importance. Hence, researchers focused on a global description of the regularity of the function of f in form of
multifractal spectrum (also called the singularity spectrum) reﬂecting the size of the set of points for which the Holder
exponent takes a certain value h. The measure of size most commonly used is the Hausdorff dimension which gives
rise to the Hausdorff spectrum (called the multifractal spectrum). It describes the collection of Holder exponents hptq
by mapping to each value of h the Hausdorff dimension Dphq of the collection of points ti at which hfptiq  h. The
main idea being that the relative frequency of the local exponents can be represented by a renormalised density called
the multifractal spectrum.
