11.2.3
The multifractal ﬂuctuation analysis
We described in Section (3.2.2.2) the most common measures of variability when summarising the data. When studying the response time distributions of ﬁnancial return, we saw in Section (10.4.3) that some authors considered the
mean absolute price change (∆X where X is a logarithmic price) as their main scaling parameter, while other considered the square root of the variance and the interquartile range of the distribution of ∆X. An alternative approach
is to directly work with the process X and to detrend it with a polynomial, or an averaging function and measure the
error with the local root mean square.
