13.4.2
The case of inﬁnite outcome space
When |O| is large or inﬁnite, calculating the cost of a purchase becomes intractable. To address this problem Abernethy
et al. [2012] restricted the market maker to offer only K securities for some reasonably sized K, and assumed that the
payoff of each security could be described by an arbitrary (but computable) function ρ : O Ñ Rn
¥0. Such a security
space is called complex. They formalised the properties of a reasonable market, and precisely characterised the set of
all cost functions satisfying these conditions. They obtained the following conditions
